---
import ArticleLayout from '../../components/ArticleLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Who pays for repairs found in a home inspection?',
    a: 'It is negotiated. After the inspection, the buyer can request repairs, a price reduction, or a closing credit. The seller can agree, counter, or decline. Who pays depends on the market, the contract, and how the negotiation goes, nothing is automatically the seller’s responsibility.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are the most common home inspection repairs?',
    a: 'Roof and flashing issues, HVAC problems, plumbing leaks, electrical safety items (missing GFCIs, panel issues), water intrusion and grading, and rotted wood or failed caulk. These show up on the majority of inspection reports.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How fast do inspection repairs need to be done?',
    a: 'On a contract timeline, usually days, not weeks. The repair amendment sets a deadline tied to closing, so the work and the documentation both have to be finished before the closing date, which is why fast, coordinated repair help matters at this stage.',
  },
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  slug="home-inspection-repair-cost"
  eyebrow="Cost Guide"
  title="How Much Do Home Inspection Repairs Cost? (For Buyers, Sellers & Agents)"
  description="What the common inspection repair items actually cost, how repair negotiations work, and how to keep a closing on schedule when the report lands."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    Most negotiated home inspection repairs total <strong>a few hundred to a
    few thousand dollars</strong>, though a major roof, HVAC, or foundation
    finding can run well beyond that. The report itself is just a list; what
    matters is what each item costs to fix and who ends up paying. Here is
    what the common items run, and how the repair negotiation actually works.
  </p>

  <h2>What common inspection repairs cost</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Inspection finding</th><th>Typical repair cost</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Missing GFCI outlets</td><td>$100 – $300</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Minor plumbing leak</td><td>$150 – $500</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Water heater replacement</td><td>$1,000 – $2,500</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Roof repair (localized)</td><td>$400 – $1,500</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HVAC repair</td><td>$300 – $2,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rotted trim / fascia</td><td>$300 – $1,200</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Regrade / drainage fix</td><td>$500 – $3,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Caulk, seals, minor punch list</td><td>$150 – $600</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How repair negotiations work</h2>
  <p>
    After the inspection, the buyer typically responds with a repair request,
    a price reduction, or a closing credit. The seller can accept, counter, or
    decline. Nothing is automatically anyone's responsibility, it is a
    negotiation shaped by the market and the contract. The side that comes to
    that conversation with <strong>a real line-item repair quote</strong>
    negotiates from facts while the other side is still guessing, and that
    usually wins.
  </p>

  <h2>The deadline is the hard part</h2>
  <div class="takeaway">
    <p>
      Repair amendments run on the closing clock, usually days, not weeks. The
      work has to be done and documented before closing. That is why a repair
      list two weeks out becomes a scramble: the contractors who could do it
      are booked, and a slipped repair can slip the whole closing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>For agents: this is a relationship, not a task</h2>
  <p>
    Agents who never sweat inspection week have one thing in common, a
    standing repair partner who quotes from the report, schedules against the
    closing date, and hands back photos and an itemized invoice for the
    amendment. That relationship protects the timeline, the client, and the
    agent's referral reputation. We wrote a full piece on it:
    <a href="/articles/inspection-repairs-for-real-estate-agents/">why every
    agent needs a repair partner in their rolodex</a>, and it is exactly how
    we work with <a href="/real-estate-agents/">agents across Middle
    Tennessee</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Budget a few hundred to a few thousand for a typical repair list, more for
    a major-system finding, and know that the negotiation favors whoever has
    real numbers first. Upkeep quotes inspection repairs line by line from the
    report and schedules against your closing date. One
    <a href="/work-order/">service request</a> with the report attached gets
    it moving.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
